X:1
T:Y Perot ar y Pren Pêr
T:The Parrot on the Pear Tree
R:cân
S:https://amgueddfa.cymru/casgliadau/caneuongwerin/?id=20 (collected by D. Roy Saer)
N:Sung by John Thomas (born 1912), an engineer, of Felin, Abercastell, near Mathri, north Pembrokeshire; recorded 18.9.63 (St Fagans tape 609). He learnt it at about twelve from his uncle Ben Phillips, "Ben Bach" (1871-1958), the well-known folk singer. Only stanzas 1, 2, 3 and 12 were recorded.
N:A Welsh relative of "The Twelve Days of Christmas". It was probably once a Christmas or Twelfth Night game or feat song (cân orchest): the list of gifts was sung on one breath, faster and faster as the stanzas grew.
N:Stanza 1 is the first line of music. For stanzas 2-12, sing the second line, then the gifts from that stanza's number (the numbers over the bars) down to 2, then the last two bars. The first bar of the stanza 2-12 tune is also printed with one crotchet for "ail" in place of the first two quavers. In stanzas 4 and 12 the pickup is a triplet of three semiquavers, (3D/D/D/; for stanza 11 ("Ar yr unfed dydd ar ddeg o'r...") the score suggests starting D/D/ | GGGG GG |, a bar of six quavers.
